+(duration-opts)=
+
+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a human-readable duration into a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. A value is either a
+whole number of seconds, such as `120`, or one or more integer and unit pairs using the units `d` (days), `h` (hours),
+`m` (minutes), and `s` (seconds), such as `2d`, `1h30m`, or `1d2h3m4s`. Each unit may appear at most once, units must be
+given in that order, and spaces are not allowed. Units are case-insensitive, so `45S` is the same as `45s`. A
+{class}`~datetime.timedelta` passed as a default or from Python is returned unchanged.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default="30s")
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {timeout.total_seconds():g} seconds.")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=["--timeout=1h30m"])
+    invoke(wait, args=["--timeout=90"])
+    invoke(wait, args=["--timeout=30m1h"])
+    invoke(wait, args=["--help"])
+```
+
